    <help><![CDATA[

@MOTHUR_OVERVIEW@


**Command Documentation**

The cluster.split_ command assigns sequences to OTUs (Operational Taxonomic Units).

Since mothur 1.46, the command works in two steps: first the sequences are split
into groups sharing the same taxonomy up to the ``taxlevel`` level, and the
pairwise distances below the ``cutoff`` are calculated for each group; then the
sequences are clustered within each group using the selected ``method``.
Sequences without a close enough partner end up as singletons.

With a name file input, the tool outputs the OTU list (``.list``), the rank
abundance (``.rabund``) and the species abundance (``.sabund``) files; with a
count table input, only the OTU list is produced.

The VSEARCH methods (agc and dgc) require aligned sequences. The ``classic``
option runs the clustering with cluster.classic and cannot be combined with agc
or dgc.

If ``perform clustering?`` is off, the tool stops after the splitting step and
outputs the per-group distance files, name files, the singletons and a
``.file`` summary. Note that the ``.file`` references the temporary files by the
names they had in the tool's working directory, so it can only be used to
resume a split from within the same directory; it cannot be passed back into
this tool from Galaxy.

.. _cluster.split: https://mothur.org/wiki/Cluster.split

**Input requirements**

The fasta file must contain aligned sequences of equal length; the distance calculation for each
taxonomy group aborts with ``[ERROR]: your sequences are not the same length, aborting.``
otherwise, and mothur may die without any further message (exit status 139, reported as a failed
job). Unexplained failures of this kind are collected in
`tools-iuc#3663 <https://github.com/galaxyproject/tools-iuc/issues/3663>`_.

**Known upstream bug**

mothur 1.48 crashes (segmentation fault) in cluster.split when none of the
taxonomy groups contains at least one pair of sequences with a distance below
the cutoff. This happens, for example, with very small input sets or with
sequences that have no close relatives at the chosen taxlevel and cutoff. The
crash is in ClusterSplitCommand::createProcesses, which indexes an empty list
of distance files. Use an input where at least one group has pairwise
distances below the cutoff, or raise the cutoff / lower the taxlevel if the
job fails.

v1.28.0: Upgraded to Mothur 1.33, introduced cluster boolean.

v1.48.6: Updated to the mothur 1.48 interface. The splitmethod/column/phylip
options (splitting an existing distance matrix) and the large option no longer
exist; the command now requires a fasta file, a taxonomy file and a name file
or count table. Added the weighted clustering method and the dist option; the
default method is now opti.

    ]]></help>
